all right so you've done the hard yards  you've made the design from scratch  you've spent hours on end perfecting  your design and making it exactly how  you want now it's time to export your  design in archicad 25 and be able to  actually showcase it and print it if you  want to follow along with this tutorial  i'll link it above just so you can  actually go back and recreate this if  you want but if you're simply just  trying to actually export whatever  you've documented in archicad then  follow along here we're going to start  by going to our layout book on the right  hand side of our navigator we want to be  using the view map to be able to  actually define what we need let's say  we're going to use our architectural  plans we're going to use our ground plan  so if we double click on that we'll see  that this is our ground floor plan the  same goes for our sections and  elevations than anything else we've  created so in this point in time i'm  simply going to go command d move all of  those roughly somewhere over here so  that we have elevation sections and  everything cutting through the building  as if we were actually documenting this  project step by step now if we come  across to our layout book you'll see  that predominantly we already have a  million and one things already set up  now you can go ahead and use any of  these over here to actually create what  you want i have personally created my  own archicad 25 layout book title blocks  if you want to check them out they're in  the description below but for today  we're just simply going to use the  generic export on the right hand side  now i'm going to start by creating a  whole new layout instead of going  through and using their typical layouts  just to show you exactly how the process  works so if we click on a new layout  we're not going to include it in any  automatic numbering sequences we can if  we want so let's go do not include we're  going to create a custom number a double  zero and we're going to call it the  layout we'll use our a1 landscape title  block for the purpose of this and simply  click create now that you can see it's  automatically created our a double zero  layout which for this instance let's say  we want that to be our architectural  site plan so if we click drag and let go  of our site plan we'll see that our site  plan has been automatically updated and  placed in to this elevation if we then  click on one of the external bounding  boxes we can see that we activate our  offset edges and we can slowly drag out  our site plan to the full size of this  page right click update once more to see  that we've got everything actually shown  on our page if we click on this black  dot once again and then move our sub  element it'll allow us to move the  actual image inside of that view window  rather than manipulating the view window  itself so let's just simply center that  for the time being next we're going to  repeat the same step going new layout do  not include this layout in the sequence  a01  and create and we have our next page we  can repeat that same step by going to  our ground floor plan and repeating that  step a couple more times to create  sections and elevations so i've created  two more layout books there in my  elevations i'm going to hold shift drag  all four of these across it's  automatically going to update them and  place them smack bang in the middle so  then all we have to do is adjust our  edges so we can actually create that  and have it exactly where we need to be  so we don't have to showcase too much  information that we don't need drag them  onto our page where they can fit and as  you can see now we can see our 3d color  elevation cutting through our earth  showing our grass hatch above our stone  our glass and our building obviously  there's a lot more information that  needs to go on to these drawings to be  any use to anybody in the real world but  this is just an export example finally  we can do the same thing over here for  our sections so we've got two sections  that we can drag and drop onto this pdf  adjust our settings as required command  d  move them to the center and there we  have our two sections started as we've  drawn it now you would have noticed that  i created these as individual numbered  elements so if we don't want to have to  go back and individually change these  one by one so as an example if i change  that moved it above whatever other page  i wanted it doesn't automatically  renumber them we can select all those  pages right click layout settings let's  untick this box here let's use the  automatic book settings and let's go  okay so you'll see it automatically  changes the numbering system 0 1 2 3 and  if i was to move that one below that one  it would renumber them you can also  rename these individually so go rename  layout change the layout name to  elevations because that's what it is so  you know what it is so now it makes a  bit more sense so if i drag this site  plan above that ground floor plan you'll  see that automatically numbered if you  want that automatic numbering to be a  little bit different a little bit more  applicable to how you wish to number  them we can right click on our book  settings and we can change our setting  style so let's say we want to have an a  in front we want to use double letters  instead of single click ok you'll see  all of those update automatically to the  a  a0010203 system now if you have no idea  what any of that does if we scroll into  the bottom corner of each page you'll  see that it has actual information so  ground floor plan a01 elevations a02 and  it relates to that information so when  you're flicking through a printed set  you know exactly what page people are  referring to now to export these four  pages of the pdf there's a couple  different steps we can take one of the  simplest ways is to highlight all of the  pages you wish to export by holding  shift clicking on the first and clicking  on the last going file save as let's  save that to the desktop change this to  a pdf file go document options selected  layouts in navigator making sure that  they're in color clicking ok  and then renaming to example export  let's open this file after we've done so  we can just see what it's actually  created so as you can see now it's  exported this pdf document exactly like  we saw in the archicad model we have all  our pages individually named  individually numbered that you can print  share or do whatever a second  alternative that has a little bit more  control a little bit more flexibility  and also at the same time a little bit  more of a foolproof system when you're  coming back to a drawing set so you know  exactly what pages you exported before  if we click on our project overview here  go show organizer we don't need this  batch renderer for the time being so i'm  going to delete that and go new folder  let's go example export 2  and then i'm going to drag and drop all  four of these files into that example  export too if we right click on that  example publishing properties saving  that just directly to our desktop and  clicking ok so then we can click ok  again  merge all to one pdf or you can leave  that unticked so you can have individual  pdfs some builders prefer to have  individual pdfs some people like to  export as one booklet and simply  clicking the publish button when you're  completed if i close both of those there  minimize my window i can see example set  two over here and we'll see that we've  exported exactly the same thing with  just a little bit more control anyway  that's all for me today guys thank you  so much for watching i hope you enjoyed  the video if you did make sure you smash  the subscribe button and like always  i'll see you next monday

